WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.069 --> 00:00:05.760
Dearest viewer, this author finds herself
compelled to share the most curious of news.

00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:10.410
It seems that Netflix's scandalously fashionable
show Bridgerton is positively brimming with

00:00:10.410 --> 00:00:14.790
hidden easter eggs and secret details you
might have missed, the most exhilarating of

00:00:14.790 --> 00:00:15.869
which I shall now uncover.

00:00:15.869 --> 00:00:19.300
Be forewarned, dear viewer, for there are
spoilers ahead!

00:00:19.300 --> 00:00:23.810
There are bees buzzing around everywhere in
Bridgerton, from the opening scene of the

00:00:23.810 --> 00:00:28.300
series, to the show's title sequence through
to the very final scene of Season 1.

00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:32.259
They even feature on Eloise's hair accessories
and Benedict's collar.

00:00:32.259 --> 00:00:36.760
The first bee appears on the door of the Bridgerton
household just as Lady Whistledown mentions

00:00:36.760 --> 00:00:40.670
"the widowed Viscountess",
a neat reference to how Violet became a widow

00:00:40.670 --> 00:00:44.570
after her husband died from a bee sting in
the novels that the show'is based on.

00:00:44.570 --> 00:00:49.520
Also, at the start of the final episode, when
Whistledown talks about the fastest courtship

00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:54.629
on record, notice this bee on the roses decorating
the just-married couple's carriage.

00:00:54.629 --> 00:01:00.179
"Of course, Miss Leopold and her new husband
would leave London mere hours after their

00:01:00.179 --> 00:01:01.179
wedding.

00:01:01.179 --> 00:01:02.510
Reason unknown."

00:01:02.510 --> 00:01:06.750
Given the crucial role of bees in pollination
and therefore how culturally they're linked

00:01:06.750 --> 00:01:11.120
with reproduction, this clip clearly suggests
that the couple left so hurriedly as she was

00:01:11.120 --> 00:01:13.350
pregnant or about to become so!

00:01:13.350 --> 00:01:17.321
Which is one reason why a bee appears again
at the end of the episode just after Daphne

00:01:17.321 --> 00:01:18.530
gives birth.

00:01:18.530 --> 00:01:23.140
And that last shot in the season finale is
also a big hint that, in Season 2,

00:01:23.140 --> 00:01:27.140
Anthony's story will follow what happens in
the second book where his fear of bees after

00:01:27.140 --> 00:01:32.420
his father's death leads him into a scandalous
situation with a new love interest.

00:01:32.420 --> 00:01:36.040
And bees aren't the only insects with added
meaning in the series.

00:01:36.040 --> 00:01:39.970
The Featherington family's flamboyant fashions
can be quite a lot to take in,

00:01:39.970 --> 00:01:45.630
so you may have missed the fact that butterflies
often form part of their outfits, especially

00:01:45.630 --> 00:01:50.330
Penelope's, from her various hair accessories
to several of her necklaces, and the many

00:01:50.330 --> 00:01:53.000
large and small ones on this dress here.

00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:57.430
It's certainly fitting that the show associates
butterflies with the Featheringtons as not

00:01:57.430 --> 00:02:02.030
only are the insects often highly colourful,
but also, because of their short lifespans

00:02:02.030 --> 00:02:05.940
and ephemeral beauty, they can be symbols
of empty vanity.

00:02:05.940 --> 00:02:11.350
As to why they're linked to Penelope in particular,
the fact butterflies often symbolise transformation,

00:02:11.350 --> 00:02:16.130
freedom, and new beginnings is a hint that
Penelope's not exactly what she at first appears

00:02:16.130 --> 00:02:17.130
to be.

00:02:17.130 --> 00:02:21.060
At the beginning of the show, she seems to
be a debutante that few people pay attention

00:02:21.060 --> 00:02:22.060
to.

00:02:22.060 --> 00:02:23.060
"Penelope, I did not see you there."

00:02:23.060 --> 00:02:27.640
But by the end of Season 1 our perception
of her has been transformed as we discover

00:02:27.640 --> 00:02:32.610
she's the author of society's most popular
scandal sheet whose words everyone listens

00:02:32.610 --> 00:02:33.610
to.

00:02:33.610 --> 00:02:37.660
And thanks to that secret identity, she enjoys
a level of freedom she never had before.

00:02:37.660 --> 00:02:40.650
"A scribbling woman is the most canine.

00:02:40.650 --> 00:02:45.600
If that should be true, then this author would
like to show you her teeth."

00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:49.830
When it came to dressing the Featherington
family, costume designer Ellen Mirojnick went

00:02:49.830 --> 00:02:54.810
deliberately over the top,
aiming for a "gaudy", "overdecorated", "overzealous"

00:02:54.810 --> 00:02:59.620
look to convey the fact they're nouveau riche
and to create a sharp contrast with the refinement

00:02:59.620 --> 00:03:02.570
of the more well-established Bridgerton family.

00:03:02.570 --> 00:03:07.280
So, Featherington matriarch, Portia, goes
for bright colours, bold patterns, and lots

00:03:07.280 --> 00:03:12.050
of embellishments as she wants her daughters
to stand out so she can marry them off.

00:03:12.050 --> 00:03:17.510
The Bridgerton family's look is more elegant
and muted, leaning into a pretty, pristine,

00:03:17.510 --> 00:03:21.260
powdery palette of pale blues, silvers, and
greens.

00:03:21.260 --> 00:03:26.040
And when it comes to Daphne specifically,
as her character matures, she goes for "duskier"

00:03:26.040 --> 00:03:29.130
colours, with richer pinks and blues and deeper
silvers.

00:03:29.130 --> 00:03:35.380
As costume designer Mirojnick puts it, "She
begins as a porcelain doll and becomes a woman."

00:03:35.380 --> 00:03:37.400
As for Eloise, who's smart, rebellious, and
speaks her mind,

00:03:37.400 --> 00:03:42.260
"Perhaps you should like to paint a picture?"
her clothes tend to be simple in shape and

00:03:42.260 --> 00:03:46.900
quite covered up, and rather than being "being
sent out and decorated", she gets what the

00:03:46.900 --> 00:03:49.640
designer calls a "masculine twist".

00:03:49.640 --> 00:03:54.520
Then there's Simon whose rich velvet fabrics
and brocades, as well as the colours, patterns,

00:03:54.520 --> 00:03:59.010
and cut of his clothes stand out as they're
influenced by his travels abroad, according

00:03:59.010 --> 00:04:00.070
to Mirojnick.

00:04:00.070 --> 00:04:03.880
Reds and golds, which are his family colours
in the novels, are quite often part of his

00:04:03.880 --> 00:04:06.340
outfits,
though according to the costume designer the

00:04:06.340 --> 00:04:11.210
real reason for that is that the very first
time they tried red on actor Regé-Jean Page

00:04:11.210 --> 00:04:14.230
in a fitting, he just looked so good in it.

00:04:14.230 --> 00:04:19.090
On top of that, L'OfficielUSA.com describes
Simon's "dark and mature palette" as providing

00:04:19.090 --> 00:04:24.500
a "contrast to Daphne's frothy pieces" which
points "towards the dynamic of their relationship."

00:04:24.500 --> 00:04:27.810
And, if you're wondering about the significance
of the "diamond-and-emerald encrusted enamel

00:04:27.810 --> 00:04:33.120
brooch" that Simon can often be seen wearing
on his lapel, it belonged to his late mother

00:04:33.120 --> 00:04:34.870
so acts as a reminder of her.

00:04:34.870 --> 00:04:39.720
Due to showrunner Chris Van Dusen's obsession
with the BBC adaptation of Pride &amp; Prejudice

00:04:39.720 --> 00:04:43.810
from the 1990s, Simon gets his very own Mr
Darcy-style moment,

00:04:43.810 --> 00:04:47.810
though Van Dusen "wanted to see a period piece
that went even further than that".

00:04:47.810 --> 00:04:53.250
So this time, the wet white shirt isn't caused
by plunging into a lake; instead Simon gets

00:04:53.250 --> 00:04:58.300
caught in a sudden downpour during some al-fresco
late-night loving with his new wife.

00:04:58.300 --> 00:05:03.230
Tulips are mentioned a few times in the show,
but this exchange between Violet Bridgerton

00:05:03.230 --> 00:05:05.370
and her son Anthony particularly caught my
attention:

00:05:05.370 --> 00:05:06.490
"This is for Daphne.

00:05:06.490 --> 00:05:07.490
Tulips.

00:05:07.490 --> 00:05:08.490
They symbolize passion.

00:05:08.490 --> 00:05:11.810
A most appropriate hem for your sister when
she decides to marry the duke.

00:05:11.810 --> 00:05:13.750
Perhaps your bride would like the same."

00:05:13.750 --> 00:05:18.250
This seemingly small moment is very significant
as it's a big clue to what will happen in

00:05:18.250 --> 00:05:19.930
season 2 of the show.

00:05:19.930 --> 00:05:20.930
Why's that?

00:05:20.930 --> 00:05:24.340
Well, because it's a hat-tip to the novels
where Violet has filled the garden of her

00:05:24.340 --> 00:05:27.460
country home with one of her favourite flowers,
tulips.

00:05:27.460 --> 00:05:32.060
And in the second book of the Bridgerton saga,
Anthony picks a tulip for his new love interest.

00:05:32.060 --> 00:05:36.860
So, in the first episode, during one of Anthony's
trysts with Siena, when you see his watch

00:05:36.860 --> 00:05:39.260
on this piece of furniture with a tulip-like
design,

00:05:39.260 --> 00:05:43.831
it's a subtle hint that his time with her
will soon be over as his new love interest

00:05:43.831 --> 00:05:48.340
in the second season will be the woman he
gives the tulip to in the books.

00:05:48.340 --> 00:05:52.591
When Anthony tells his youngest sister Hyacinth
she can join in with the summer fun at their

00:05:52.591 --> 00:05:55.670
country home as long as she "stays clear of
[his] lucky mallet",

00:05:55.670 --> 00:06:00.290
that's a nod to the croquet-like game of pall-mall
that the Bridgertons love to play in the novels

00:06:00.290 --> 00:06:04.520
and has become a real fan-favourite moment,
according to author Julia Quinn.

00:06:04.520 --> 00:06:08.270
That game, which in the second novel Daphne
says is full of bad sportsmanship, cheating,

00:06:08.270 --> 00:06:12.590
and lying, and "is less about winning than
making sure the other players lose",

00:06:12.590 --> 00:06:16.950
plays an important role in Anthony's love
life so it'll very likely pop up in the second

00:06:16.950 --> 00:06:22.220
season, especially as Quinn asked showrunner
Van Dusen to make sure it does!

00:06:22.220 --> 00:06:26.550
During a flashback to Simon's childhood, we
learn his father had him hidden away because

00:06:26.550 --> 00:06:27.620
of his stammer.

00:06:27.620 --> 00:06:31.800
Instead of going to school then, his governess
taught him and his lessons included Latin,

00:06:31.800 --> 00:06:33.800
which was usual for boys at the time.

00:06:33.800 --> 00:06:37.680
An interesting detail that signals the kind
of man Simon will grow up to be are the Latin

00:06:37.680 --> 00:06:41.810
conjugations on the blackboard for the word
'pugnare' which means "to fight, to battle",

00:06:41.810 --> 00:06:44.150
to "strive" or "struggle".

00:06:44.150 --> 00:06:49.150
As a child, Simon strove to overcome his stammer
and lack of confidence with the help of Lady

00:06:49.150 --> 00:06:53.720
Danbury; and as an adult, he not only gets
involved in boxing, but he also ends up in

00:06:53.720 --> 00:06:54.770
a duel with Anthony.

00:06:54.770 --> 00:06:59.900
So, this shot shows how Simon has, in a way,
been learning the meaning of fighting since

00:06:59.900 --> 00:07:01.620
he was a child.

00:07:01.620 --> 00:07:06.400
During a raunchy rendezvous, it's no coincidence
that Anthony crashes into a poster for Mozart's

00:07:06.400 --> 00:07:09.770
opera Don Giovanni on Siena's dressing room
wall.

00:07:09.770 --> 00:07:14.450
That particular opera is based on the legend
of Don Juan, a young, licentious nobleman

00:07:14.450 --> 00:07:18.870
who places pleasure above all else, which
Anthony certainly does for much of the first

00:07:18.870 --> 00:07:19.870
season.

00:07:19.870 --> 00:07:21.419
"You like to speak of responsibility.

00:07:21.419 --> 00:07:23.090
My dear son!

00:07:23.090 --> 00:07:24.510
Pray tell, what should you know of it?"

00:07:24.510 --> 00:07:28.919
The way the framed poster tumbles to the floor
could also be a little hint to how Anthony

00:07:28.919 --> 00:07:32.270
and Siena's relationship will eventually come
crashing down.

00:07:32.270 --> 00:07:36.710
The line 'for three nights only' on the poster
also foreshadows the temporary nature of their

00:07:36.710 --> 00:07:37.710
affair.

00:07:37.710 --> 00:07:41.430
There's another clever bit of foreshadowing
on the dance cards and the ballroom floor

00:07:41.430 --> 00:07:46.310
at Danbury House, where a Latin phrase appears
which means "We do not learn for school, but

00:07:46.310 --> 00:07:47.310
for life".

00:07:47.310 --> 00:07:50.990
This fits the show well because, as showrunner
Van Dusen says, Season 1 focuses on

00:07:50.990 --> 00:07:55.590
"the education of Daphne Bridgerton" who
"starts out as this young innocent debutante

00:07:55.590 --> 00:07:56.750
who knows very little of love.

00:07:56.750 --> 00:07:58.630
And [...] nothing of sex."

00:07:58.630 --> 00:08:03.790
"Titled, chaste, and innocent, this is what
they have been raised and trained for since

00:08:03.790 --> 00:08:04.790
birth."

00:08:04.790 --> 00:08:09.150
Then, as Van Dusen says, "over the course
of the series, we watch her transform entirely."

00:08:09.150 --> 00:08:11.540
Now, what did you think of Bridgerton?

00:08:11.540 --> 00:08:13.900
And did you notice any little details or easter
eggs?

00:08:13.900 --> 00:08:15.710
Leave your comments below!

00:08:15.710 --> 00:08:20.080
And if you enjoyed this, don't forget to leave
a thumbs-up, it's hugely appreciated.

